exec(open('/tmp/security04-helper.py').read());n='web-sop-cors'
md(n,'how CORS deliberately relaxes that rule without opening a hole','how CORS grants controlled access and how misconfiguration exposes data','CORS configuration can introduce vulnerabilities.')
md(n,'An **origin** is the triple of **scheme, host and port**. Two URLs are same-origin only if all three match exactly.','For ordinary HTTP(S) URLs, an **origin** compares the normalised **scheme, host and port**; an explicit default port and its omitted form are equivalent. Documents can instead have opaque origins, including sandboxed documents without same-origin permission. Identical `null` serialisations do not make distinct opaque origins equal.', 'Tuple model needs normalisation and opaque-origin exceptions.')
md(n,'A looser idea, the **site**, is the scheme plus the *registrable domain*','For the domain-based HTTP(S) examples here, a looser idea, the **schemeful site**, is the scheme plus the *registrable domain*','IP hosts and opaque origins need different site computation.')
md(n,'The page can *use* them but not inspect their raw bytes.','Resource type, CORS mode, CSP, CORP/COEP, framing policy and other checks affect loading. Classic scripts differ from module scripts, which require CORS for cross-origin loading. Successful embedding alone does not grant unrestricted response-byte access.', 'Embedding is not universally allowed and CORS mode matters.')
md(n,'Script cannot read a cross-origin `fetch` response, the DOM of a cross-origin iframe, or the pixels of a cross-origin image drawn on a canvas.','Without the applicable permission, script cannot read a cross-origin `fetch` response or iframe DOM. Drawing an image without the required CORS permission can taint a canvas and prevent pixel readback. CORS-approved images can preserve readback.', 'Canvas and fetch have explicit permission mechanisms.')
md(n,'The request still reaches the server and any side effects still happen.','A simple request can reach the server even when its response is unreadable. A failed required preflight can prevent the actual request; other browser policies can block sending too.', 'Avoid contradicting preflight enforcement and claiming guaranteed side effects.')
md(n,'The browser adds an `Origin` header to cross-origin requests.','A browser CORS fetch includes an `Origin` header; not every cross-origin navigation or embedding request does.','Origin emission depends on request type/mode.')
# Actual single-line HTTP fields fit using short reserved example origins.
md(n,'https://app.example','https://a.test','Short reserved origin permits valid wire-format headers within44character lesson limit.')
md(n,'https://api.example','https://b.test','Keep counterpart example distinct with valid short header values.')
md(n,'Access-Control-Allow-Origin:\n  https://a.test','Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://a.test','HTTP field name and value cannot be taught as obsolete line folding.')
md(n,'Access-Control-Request-Headers:\n  authorization','Access-Control-Request-Headers: authorization','Use valid single-line wire syntax.')
md(n,'Access-Control-Allow-Methods:\n  GET, POST, DELETE','Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, DELETE','Use valid single-line wire syntax.')
md(n,'Access-Control-Allow-Headers:\n  authorization','Access-Control-Allow-Headers: authorization','Use valid single-line wire syntax.')
md(n,'If `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` matches the calling origin (or is `*`), the browser hands the response to the script. Otherwise the script sees a network error. CORS never makes anything *more* restrictive; it only relaxes the SOP.','For a CORS fetch, a matching allow-origin value is necessary to expose the response; `*` works only when credentials mode is not `include`. Credentialed requests also require allow-credentials. CORS failures are exposed as network errors. A required preflight additionally gates sending the actual method/headers; CORS is not server authentication.', 'Origin match alone is not sufficient and preflight is a sending gate.')
md(n,'Some requests could already be made by an ordinary HTML form before CORS existed, so CORS cannot make them more dangerous. These **simple requests** are sent directly:','The safelist preserves forms of cross-origin traffic historically possible without CORS. A request avoiding preflight still can have harmful side effects. The following is a simplified checklist for a CORS fetch that does not otherwise require preflight:', 'Historical rationale does not mean request harmlessness.')
md(n,'- Only "CORS-safelisted" headers (such as `Accept` and `Content-Type`).','- Author-set headers meet the CORS safelist, including header-specific byte, length and syntax restrictions; a safelisted name alone is insufficient.', 'Fetch header safelist checks values too.')
md(n,'Anything else (a `PUT` or `DELETE`, an `Authorization` header, `Content-Type: application/json`) triggers a **preflight**: the browser first sends an `OPTIONS` request asking permission.','Methods such as `PUT`/`DELETE`, an author-set `Authorization` header or JSON content type require preflight permission in a CORS fetch. Without a matching unexpired preflight-cache entry, the browser sends `OPTIONS` first. Streaming bodies or XHR upload listeners can require preflight too.', 'Cached permissions and API preflight flags qualify always-OPTIONS claim.')
md(n,'Only if the preflight passes does the browser send the real `DELETE`. `Access-Control-Max-Age` lets the browser cache the answer; browsers cap it (Chromium at 2 hours, Firefox at 24 hours) and default to 5 seconds if it is absent.','The actual `DELETE` needs successful preflight permission. Fetch specifies a five-second default when max-age is missing or invalid, with implementation caps and possible earlier eviction. Chromium source currently uses a two-hour maximum; this is version-specific, not guaranteed retention or a cache shared with ordinary HTTP responses.', 'Avoid unverified Firefox current cap and guaranteed retention; primary Chromium code checked.')
md(n,'any site can now read logged-in users\' data.','an attacker-controlled origin can read a sensitive response if credentials are actually eligible and sent and the endpoint returns the data.', 'CORS allowlisting does not override cookie/authentication policy.')
md(n,'Vulnerable (Flask):','Vulnerable Flask hook fragment (assume `app` and `request` are provided; do not deploy it):','Make setup dependency and deliberately unsafe example explicit.')
md(n,'Fixed, with an exact allowlist:','Response-sharing hook with an exact allowlist (same setup assumptions; this is not a complete authentication or preflight handler):','Response hook alone does not implement preflight methods/header checks.')
md(n,'resp.headers["Vary"] = "Origin"','resp.vary.add("Origin")','Preserve existingVary dimensions such asAccept-Encoding rather than overwriting them.')
md(n,'// receiver: check who sent it','// receiver: assume trusted parent window','Receiver needs source identity and message schema as well as origin.')
md(n,'  handle(e.data);','  if (e.source !== parent) return;\n  if (typeof e.data !== "string") return;\n  handle(e.data);','Restrict senderwindow and simple expectedmessage type; handler remains trusted application code.')
md(n,'skipping the `e.origin` check lets any page drive your handler.','an unchecked handler can accept messages from an untrusted window that has a reference to it. Validate origin, expected source and message structure, and treat the payload as untrusted data.','Not literally every page has a windowreference; origin alone doesnotvalidate data.')
md(n,'| `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` | Who may embed this resource |','| `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` | Restricts applicable cross-origin no-cors resource loads |','CORP doesnotgovern everyembedding/CORSresponse.')
md(n,'| `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` | Cut the link to cross-origin popups |','| `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` | Controls browsing-context-group isolation for top-level documents |','COOP policies have different openerrelationships; not blanket severing.')
md(n,'| `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` | Only load opted-in resources |','| `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` | Controls embedding; require-corp and credentialless have different rules |','Credentialless permits some credential-free no-cors loads without CORP.')
md(n,'CORS is the server opting in to let named origins read responses. It relaxes the SOP; it never protects the server.','CORS controls browser response sharing and preflight permission. It does not replace server authentication, authorisation or CSRF defences.','Preflight has meaningful gating but is not general serverprotection.')
md(n,'Non-simple requests (JSON bodies, custom headers, `PUT`/`DELETE`) are preflighted with `OPTIONS`.','Non-safelisted CORS requests need preflight permission; cached permission can avoid another OPTIONS exchange.','Keep takeaway consistent with preflight cache.')
p=r/(n+'.md');s=p.read_text().replace('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Evidence limits\n> Browser-specific preflight retention, third-party-cookie policy and legacy origin relaxation vary by version and configuration. A complete browser compatibility matrix and universal exploit-success rates are absent because they were not reliably established. Wire examples are abbreviated; the Flask hooks need an application, authentication, cache policy and preflight handling.\n\n## Key takeaways');p.write_text(s)
